Here's a JavaScript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries from an object where the nested key-value pair matches the provided string key:

```javascript
function getValuesByKey(obj, key) {
  const keys = key.split(".");
  let value = obj;
  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length - 1; i++) {
    value = value[keys[i]];
    if (typeof value === "undefined") {
      return [];
    }
  }
  const lastKey = keys[keys.length - 1];
  const values = [];
  Object.values(value).forEach((item) => {
    if (item[lastKey] === obj[key]) {
      values.push(item);
    }
  });
  return values;
}
```

To use this function, pass in your object and the key you want to search by in dot notation. The function will return an array of objects containing the matched value for the specified key. Here's an example usage:

```javascript
const myObj = {
  user: {
    name: "John Doe",
    age: 25,
    addresses: {
      home: { street: "123 Main St" },
      work: { street: "456 Elm St" }
    }
  }
};

const homeAddresses = getValuesByKey(myObj, "user.addresses.home.street");
console.log(homeAddresses); // Output: ["123 Main St"]
```

In this example, we pass `myObj`, the main object we want to search through, and `"user.addresses.home.street"` as the key to search by. The function then returns an array containing only the `street` property values from any nested object matching our key. In this case, we get back `["123 Main St"]`. If there were no matches, the function would return an empty array instead.

